====Synonyms====
* {{l|en|justiciary}}

====Usage notes====
Many editors would maintain the differentiation between {{m|en|judicial}} and {{m|en|judicious}} and thus would advise that writers should not confuse {{m|en|judicial}} (having to do with justice and judiciary systems) with {{m|en|judicious}} (showing good judgment). In adhering to this prescription, a phrase such as ''judicious use of X'' (wisely chosen use of X) is not interchangeable with ''judicial use of X'' (use of X by the courts). Descriptively, {{m|en|judicial}} is [[polysemy#Noun|polysemic]], with one of its senses meaning {{m|en|judicious}}, but the purpose of the prescription is to avoid having some readers sense awkwardness or apparent [[catachresis]] in the poorly chosen (albeit descriptively not wrong) usage; the editorial idea is that regardless of correctness or incorrectness, it is ''preferable'' to use the better/clearer word instead and thus avoid any chance of reader annoyance or reader confusion/missense. Meanwhile, the word {{m|en|juridical}} is not well differentiated in meaning from {{m|en|judicial}}, the two having substantial semantic overlap.

